# Optional: only needed to persist quotes and rate edits across Vercel's
# serverless instances. Without these, the app falls back to writing local
# JSON files (fine for local dev; on Vercel it falls back to /tmp, which is
# NOT shared between instances - saved quotes may 404 for other requests).
# This also means the daily exchange-rate cron job below needs these set to
# reliably reach the rest of the running app in production.
#
# Set these up in ~1 minute: Vercel dashboard -> your project -> Storage tab
# -> Create Database -> a Redis option (e.g. "Upstash for Redis") -> Connect
# to this project. Vercel then injects these automatically - you usually
# don't need to copy/paste them by hand.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# Optional: protects /api/cron/exchange-rate (see vercel.json), which
# refreshes the USD -> KRW rate every morning. Leave this unset and the
# route works out of the box with no setup - the endpoint only refreshes a
# public market rate, so it's fine left open. To restrict it to Vercel's own
# scheduled job, generate any random string (e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`)
# and set it under Vercel Project Settings -> Environment Variables - Vercel
# then automatically sends it as the Authorization header on its own cron
# requests.
CRON_SECRET=

# Required in production - signs the session cookie issued by
# /api/auth/login (HMAC-SHA256, via the Web Crypto API so it works in both
# the Edge middleware and Node API routes with no extra package). Unlike
# CRON_SECRET, this guards real login sessions, so the app refuses to sign
# sessions with a shared fallback secret in production - a dev-only default
# is used automatically when this is unset locally, so local dev needs no
# extra setup. Generate one with `openssl rand -hex 32` and set it under
# Vercel Project Settings -> Environment Variables before deploying.
AUTH_SECRET=
